{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>you shall not eat of it</b> — He warns him concerning the fruit, for the tree itself is not eaten. And so it says below (Genesis 3:3): \"from the fruit of the tree that is in the midst of the garden,\" and similarly: \"and let every man eat of his vine and of his fig tree\" (II Kings 18:31), and likewise: \"in toil you shall eat of it\" (Genesis 3:17) — you shall eat its fruit." "<b>on the day you eat of it you shall surely die</b> — At the time you eat of it, you shall be subject to death, and similarly: \"on the day you go forth and walk here and there, you shall know that you shall surely die\" (I Kings 2:42), where the intention is not that he would die immediately on that very day, nor is the intention merely knowledge, that he should know he will die — for all the living know that they will die — but rather the intention is that at the time he goes forth he will be liable to the king for death, and the king will put him to death whenever he wishes. \"And they shall not come to see when the holy is swallowed up, and die\" (Numbers 4:20); \"and they shall not bear upon it a sin and die in it when they profane it\" (Leviticus 22:9) — their meaning is only that they shall be liable to death, and they shall die in this sin of theirs.\n\nNow according to the opinion of the natural philosophers, man was predisposed to death from the beginning of his formation, because he is composite; but He decreed now that if he sins, he will die in his sin in the manner of those liable to death at the hands of Heaven for transgressions — such as a layman eating terumah (Leviticus 22:9), one intoxicated by wine (Leviticus 10:9), one lacking garments who served (Exodus 28:43), and others like them — whose meaning is that they will die in their sin before their day arrives. And therefore He said in the punishment: \"until you return to the ground, for from it you were taken, for you are dust and to dust you shall return\" (Genesis 3:19) — to your nature.\n\nFurthermore, even at the beginning he ate of the fruit of the tree and of the seed of the earth; if so, there was already in him the process and cause of coming-to-be and perishing.\n\nBut according to the opinion of our Sages (see Shabbat 55), had he not sinned he would never have died, for the supernal soul grants him eternal life, and the divine desire that is in him at the time of formation will cleave to him continually and will sustain him forever, as I explained in the phrase \"and Elohim saw that it was good\" (above, Genesis 1:4).\n\nAnd know that the composite nature indicates perishing only according to the view of those of little faith, who hold that creation is necessary; but according to the view of the people of faith who say that the world was created anew by simple divine desire, its continuation would also endure forever, all the days of that desire, and this is a clear truth. If so, \"on the day you eat of it you shall surely die\" — then you will become subject to death; you will not endure forever by My will. And the eating at the beginning was for him for pleasure.\n\nAnd it is possible that the fruits of the Garden of Eden were absorbed into the limbs like the manna and sustained those who ate them; and when He decreed upon him: \"and you shall eat the herb of the field\" (Genesis 3:18), and \"by the sweat of your brow you shall eat bread\" of the earth, this became a cause of perishing, for he is dust, and dust he will eat, and to dust he will return."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {1 "The citation in the paragraph on natural philosophers reads ויקרא כט ט (Leviticus 29:9), which does not exist; this is a copyist's error for ויקרא כב ט (Leviticus 22:9), the same verse cited correctly in the preceding line. Translated as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:20:58.240384869Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}